@startuml chooser_keys
title paddock: the chooser's screens, and the keys between them

skinparam shadowing false
hide empty description

[*] --> Form : the popup opens on the answers,\nfilled in from the profile this\nworkspace launched last

state Form : one field per decision, the hint for the\none the cursor is on, then Launch and Cancel
state "List" as Pick : one row per choice, what it means beside it
state "Checklist" as Tick : as many columns as the width holds,\nand a count in the header
state "Box" as Type : one line to type, and what is wrong with it
state Confirm : the resolved policy in full, over\nLaunch, Back to the form and Cancel.\n`s` saves the answers from here

Form --> Pick : enter on Open, Profile, Backend, Agent or Files
Form --> Tick : enter on Tools, Network or Skills
Form --> Type : enter on a name or a typed command
Pick --> Form : enter takes the row\nesc, or the Back row, takes nothing:\nthe old value stands
Tick --> Form : enter, esc or the Back row,\nand the ticks stand either way
Type --> Form : enter when the check passes\nesc keeps what was typed
Form --> Confirm : L, or enter on Launch,\nfor a sandbox
Form --> [*] : L on a local or an attached tab:\nno policy, so nothing to confirm\nesc, or enter on Cancel: nothing at all
Confirm --> [*] : Launch: one plan\nCancel: nothing at all
Confirm --> Form : Back to the form, or esc,\nwith every answer where it was

note right of Tick
  `/` opens a filter on a list and on a
  checklist. Bare typing never filters, so
  every letter stays a shortcut: that is why
  a checklist can keep `a` for all and `n`
  for none while still narrowing.

  The popup is 70% of the terminal minus its
  sidebar, so an ordinary one is about 48 by 18.
  Every screen scrolls its rows rather than
  clipping them, and pins what must never scroll
  off: the cursor, the hint, what you have typed,
  and the confirm's own three buttons.
end note

note right of Type
  Every list and every checklist draws the way
  back as its first row, so escape has something
  on screen saying what it does. The form has no
  such row: nothing is before it, and its key
  line says cancel rather than back.

  `?` puts the whole key map over whatever is
  on screen, and any key takes it off again.

  ctrl-c cancels the popup from any of these,
  at any depth, and from under the key map too,
  by raising KeyboardInterrupt.
  Nothing has been launched or written by
  then, so backing out costs nothing.

  screen.py draws these and knows nothing
  about profiles, sessions or plans. It takes
  rows of text and hands back what was picked.
  tui.py drives it.
end note

@enduml
